OpenMagicka fork of MojoShader (altered source, see native/README.md).

Preshaders: support the dotswiz6 and dotswiz8 opcodes, and never write past an instruction's
operand array.

D3DX preshaders have two dot-product opcodes MojoShader did not know, 0x70E0 (dotswiz6: the
first three scalar sources dotted with the last three) and 0x70D0 (dotswiz8: four with four).
They have 7 and 9 operands with the destination, but the instruction structure held only 4, so
parsing ArcaneEffect (whose pixel shader normalises a vector with dotswiz6 and rsq) wrote past
the array, corrupted the heap and crashed when the context was destroyed (an AddressSanitizer
build reported a bad free in MOJOSHADER_freePreshader). Now:
- the opcodes are parsed and run (MOJOSHADER_PRESHADEROP_DOTSWIZ6/8, added after the existing
  ones so no value moves; they are not "scalar ops");
- an instruction holds up to 9 operands, and one with more (or a count that wraps) fails the
  parse instead of overflowing;
- dotswiz is only accepted with one result element, the only form seen and verified;
- the preshader runner has room for 8 sources;
- testparse names the new opcodes and no longer reads past its xyzw mask when printing an
  operand that starts at w (an existing bug of the tool, not of the library).
Checked: all 51 effects of the game parse cleanly under AddressSanitizer (spirv, glsl120 and d3d
profiles), and ArcaneEffect's preshader turns 1000 random inputs into unit vectors.

